= Jasmine Flowers (Mallikā / Jātī / Yāsamīn / 茉莉花)

_The genus_ Jasminum _(Oleaceae) — multiple species treated as classical drugs across the Asian and Mediterranean traditions, each with its own pharmacology, ritual use, and cultivation register. The Sanskrit canon distinguishes at least three species; the Persian-Arabic tradition gives the West its name (yāsamīn → English_ jasmine_). Ayurvedic status: classical (multiple species named in Caraka and Suśruta). Chinese: classical-as-flower (茉莉花 mòlìhuā in late-Qing and modern pharmacology), with the canonical Chinese identification reading_ Jasminum sambac _via the Buddhist-period Sanskrit loanword 茉莉 ← mallikā. Greco-Roman: not classical — jasmine enters European pharmacy in the medieval period via Persian-Arabic transmission. Modern Western: the substantive register is_ perfumery _(jasmine absolute from_ J. grandiflorum_, Grasse and Egypt), with limited but real medicinal use. This entry covers the genus broadly with the species distinctions made explicit; for the green-tea scenting application, see jasmine-tea.typ._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*The major medicinal / cultural species:*

1. *_Jasminum sambac_ (L.) Aiton* — *Arabian / Sambac jasmine*, *sampaguita* (Philippines), *mallikā* (Sanskrit), *mòlì* 茉莉 (Chinese). Small white flowers; the *tea-scenting jasmine*; the *Hindu mālā / garland jasmine*; the *Philippine national flower*. The species the world receives as "jasmine" in southeast Asian and Chinese contexts.

2. *_Jasminum grandiflorum_ L.* (= _J. officinale_ subsp. _grandiflorum_ in some treatments) — *Spanish / royal / Catalan jasmine*, *jātī* (Sanskrit), *chameli* (Hindi), *yasmin-i Shahi* (Persian "royal jasmine"). Larger flowers; *the perfumery jasmine* — the species that supplies *jasmine absolute*, the cornerstone of classical French perfumery (Grasse) and Egyptian-Indian commercial production.

3. *_Jasminum officinale_ L.* — *common / poet's jasmine*, the Persian-Mediterranean species; cold-hardier than the others; the European garden classic. *Yāsamīn* (يَاسَمِين / یاسمن) — the original Persian-Arabic name that travelled west.

4. *_Jasminum auriculatum_ Vahl* — *jūhī* / *yūthikā* (Sanskrit), small-flowered Indian jasmine; classical Indian drug, distinct from mallikā.

5. *_Jasminum multiflorum_ (Burm.f.) Andrews* — *kunda* (Sanskrit, sometimes), winter-flowering star jasmine; minor classical.

6. *_Jasminum arborescens_ Roxb.* — *vana-mallikā* (Sanskrit), "forest jasmine," tree-form; minor classical.

*Latin / Botanical:* _Jasminum_ from Latinized _yāsamīn_; the species epithets reflect cultivation history — _sambac_ from Arabic _zanbaq_ (originally "lily" then transferred to jasmine); _grandiflorum_ "large-flowered"; _officinale_ "of the pharmacy storeroom"; _auriculatum_ "with little ears" (of the leaf base).

*English:* _Jasmine_ (also _jessamine_ in older English, preserved in literary usage) — from Old French _jasmin / jessemin_, from Arabic _yāsamīn_, from Persian _yāsamīn / yāsmīn_ (یاسمن). The Persian-to-Arabic-to-medieval-Latin-to-French-to-English chain is unbroken and is the classical Western transmission route for cultural goods from greater Iran. The word entered English ~1500 CE.#footnote[Etymology: _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "jasmine, n."); the Persian-Arabic-medieval-Romance chain is the standard derivation. The variant _jessamine_ preserves an older borrowing route.]

*Persian:* یاسمن (_yāsamīn_, _yāsmīn_) — the original Iranian-name source of the Western word family; the Persian poetic tradition (Hafez, Rumi, Sa'di) uses jasmine as an iconic flower of love, paradise, and femininity, with frequent metaphor mapping a beloved's face to a jasmine flower (_chehre yāsamīn_).

*Arabic:* يَاسَمِين (_yāsamīn_) — adopted from Persian; central in classical Arabic poetry and the medieval Arabic medical-pharmacy tradition (Avicenna's _Canon_).

*Sanskrit:* The classical Indian tradition distinguishes *multiple jasmines* by Sanskrit name, each with its own dravyaguna entry:
- *मल्लिका (mallikā)* — _Jasminum sambac_. The most-cited jasmine in Hindu ritual (mālā / garland, temple offering); Goddess Sarasvati and Lakshmi are iconographically adorned with mallikā. Caraka and Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu entries.
- *जाती (jātī)* — _Jasminum grandiflorum_. The *perfumery jasmine*; classical literary register. _Jātī-puṣpa_ (jasmine flower) and _jātī-pattra_ (the leaves; note: in Indian commerce, _jātī-pattra_ also refers to *mace*, the aril of _Myristica fragrans_ — a major source of confusion. The two are unrelated.)
- *युथी (yūthikā), जूही (jūhī)* — _Jasminum auriculatum_. Distinct medicinal use.
- *वार्षिकी (vārṣikī)* — "monsoon-flowering" — sometimes mallikā, sometimes a related species.
- *नवमल्लिका (navamallikā)* — "new jasmine" — _Jasminum multiflorum_ or related.
- *सेवन्ती (sevantī)*, *कुन्द (kunda)* — small-flowered winter species (kunda); often listed.
Synonym sets vary; the modern dravyaguna consensus (P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_) maps mallikā = sambac and jātī = grandiflorum.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _mallikā_, _jātī_, _yūthikā_, _kunda_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Puṣpa-varga_ (flower section); P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II — _mallikā_ and _jātī_ entries with the modern species-identification consensus.]

*Chinese:* 茉莉花 (mòlìhuā) — _Jasminum sambac_; the canonical Chinese jasmine. The 茉莉 character pair is a Buddhist-period transliteration of Sanskrit _mallikā_; the term has no native Chinese semantic content. 雙瓣茉莉 (shuāngbàn mòlì, "double-petalled jasmine") = the double cultivar used in premium tea-scenting. 素馨花 (sùxīnhuā, "elegant-fragrance flower") = _Jasminum officinale_ / _grandiflorum_ in Chinese — a *different* drug, used as an aromatic Liver-qi-resolving and skin-clearing flower, with its own pharmacology.#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_; the 茉莉 / 素馨 distinction is real, and modern Chinese pharmacology treats them as distinct drugs.]

*Filipino (Tagalog):* _sampaguita_ — from Spanish _sampaga_ from Sanskrit _campaka_? More likely a coastal-Asian transmission. The Philippines made _sampaguita_ the national flower in 1934; ritual use in the Holy-Week _flores de Mayo_ procession; everyday use as flower garlands sold by children at intersections.

*Indonesian / Malay:* _melati_ (Indonesian); _melur_ (Malay) — _J. sambac_; *Indonesian national flower* alongside others.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

茉莉花 enters formal Chinese pharmacology in the late-Qing and modern Chinese herbal medicine literature; the Bencao Gangmu (1578) treats jasmine only marginally as a foreign import flower. The substantive Chinese pharmacology develops in the 19th–20th c. as the flower acquires medicinal status alongside its tea-scenting role.#footnote[茉莉花 in modern Chinese materia medica: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., treats 茉莉花 in the modern register; the late-Qing entry develops as flower-pharmacy formalizes after the Bencao Gangmu. The Tang-period import status is documented in Schafer, _Golden Peaches of Samarkand_.]

*Property (性):* Warm (溫). *Flavor (味):* Pungent (辛), sweet (甘). *Channels (歸經):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Liver (肝).

*Actions & indications:*
- 理氣解鬱 — *regulates qi and resolves stagnation* — *the principal action*, especially for Liver-qi constraint with emotional / dyspeptic component (breast distension with PMS, irritability, sighing, mild depression).
- 和中辟穢 — harmonizes the middle, disperses turbid odors.
- 開胃 — opens appetite.
- 散結 — disperses nodules (gentle).
- *Topical / external:* jasmine flower decoction for chronic skin disease, conjunctivitis.

*Cautions:* dry-heat patterns; pregnancy (the qi-moving action; precautionary).#footnote[Action list: modern Chinese functional-medicine register; the 理氣解鬱 reading is the central classical-modern Chinese contribution.]

*素馨花 (J. officinale / grandiflorum)* in Chinese pharmacology has overlapping but slightly distinct uses: more strongly Liver-and-blood-clearing; for chronic skin disease, malar rash, mild liver-qi stagnation.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Jasmine flowers are treated in multiple saṃhitā references and in the major nighaṇṭus, with the species distinctions outlined above. *Both mallikā and jātī are classical; their dravyaguna placements are similar with mild distinctions.*#footnote[Mallikā and jātī in Caraka Saṃhitā references throughout; full dravyaguna in Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Puṣpa-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba); Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu; Rāja Nighaṇṭu. Translations: Murthy; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II.]

*Rasa:* Tikta (bitter), Kaṣāya (astringent), with Madhura (sweet) as a faint aromatic register.

*Guṇa:* Laghu (light), Snigdha (unctuous — distinctive for a flower drug, from the essential oil), Sūkṣma (subtle, penetrating — the canonical guṇa of aromatic flowers in Ayurvedic dravyaguna), Mṛdu (soft).

*Vīrya:* Śīta (cooling) — *the standout feature*. Jasmine flowers are *cooling*, distinguishing them from the warming spice-aromatics (cardamom, clove). This is what makes jasmine the canonical *Pitta-pacifying aromatic flower*.

*Vipāka:* Kaṭu (pungent), with secondary Madhura in some readings.#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of jasmine flowers: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Puṣpa-varga_; standard dravyaguna. The śīta vīrya is the distinguishing feature among aromatic-flower drugs.]

*Prabhāva:* *Manas-prasādana* — *mind-calming, mood-elevating* — is widely treated at the prabhāva level in classical Ayurvedic commentary; the action is *not* fully derivable from the bitter-astringent-cooling-light profile and is attested across the saṃhitā tradition as a specific affinity of jasmine for the mental-emotional channels. Some nighaṇṭus extend this to a specific *vāji-vājīkara* (mild aphrodisiac) prabhāva linked to the same calm-elevated affect; both readings are flagged as commentarial-interpretive but textually well-supported.#footnote[The manas-prasādana prabhāva of jasmine is universal in Ayurvedic commentary and in the cosmetic / ritual register; cf. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II; the use in royal cosmetic preparations (jātī-puṣpa-taila) and in nuptial / romantic contexts confirms the classical reading.]

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Pitta (the cooling-sweet-bitter); pacifies Kapha (the light-pungent-vipāka); aggravates Vāta in excess (the rūkṣa-aromatic tendency; less so for jātī and mallikā than for many bitter drugs, because of the snigdha-sūkṣma counterbalance).

*Karma:*
- _Manas-prasādana, ānandakara_ — mind-calming, joy-promoting; the canonical ritual / cosmetic / mood register.
- _Dāha-praśamana_ — relieves burning sensations (Pitta-cooling); useful in burning eyes, mouth ulcers, fevers.
- _Krimi-ghna_ — antimicrobial; classical use in dental disease (jasmine-flower mouthwash for ulcers and bad breath), eye disorders.
- _Vraṇa-ropaṇa_ — wound-healing (topical and internal).
- _Cakṣuṣya_ — eye-supportive.
- _Daurgandhya-nāśana_ — dispels foul odors (the cosmetic / ritual register).
- _Garbhāśaya-balya_ (in jātī specifically — the perfumery species) — uterine-tonic in modest commentarial reading.
- *Topical use* in cosmetic preparations: *jātī-puṣpa-taila* (jasmine-flower oil) for skin and hair tonification; *cooling and fragrant base* for facial preparations.
- *Dental use*: *jātī-pattra-kvātha* (note the mace-confusion above: the jasmine-leaf or jasmine-flower decoction in dental indications, distinct from the mace) for chronic mouth ulcers, periodontal disease, sore throat. *This is one of the most cited classical uses of jasmine.*
- Used in: chronic skin disease, _kuṣṭha_, dental and oral ulcers, fevers (especially Pitta-pattern), eye disorders, emotional disturbance, _amlapitta_ (gentle), as cosmetic and ritual flower.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā references throughout (especially Cikitsāsthāna chapters on _vraṇa-cikitsā_ and _mukha-roga_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Puṣpa-varga_; standard Ayurvedic clinical practice. The dental / mouth-ulcer use of jasmine is well-established in classical Indian dentistry — _danta-roga-cikitsā_.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(N) Conditional cautions:* Vāta-excess (the rūkṣa-laghu-sūkṣma tendency; correctable by snigdha vehicle); heavy Vāta-vacuity in cold-pattern depletion.
- *(F) Folk:* allergenic for some sensitive individuals (rare).
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Pregnancy:* the classical commentarial uterine-stimulant attribution of jātī raises a caution for high-dose use of essential oil; ritual / culinary use is fine; *jasmine essential oil for unsupervised internal use* is not recommended in pregnancy.
  - *Jasmine essential oil — undiluted topical and oral use:* the essential oil is concentrated; oral use should be at dilutions and under qualified guidance; topical use generally diluted in carrier oil.
  - *Skin sensitization:* rare contact dermatitis with concentrated jasmine essence (perfumery-grade).
  - *Drug interactions:* the linalool fraction has mild sedative pharmacology; theoretical additive effect with sedatives, benzodiazepines, alcohol.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Jasmine is canonical in Tibb under the Arabic-Persian name_ yāsamīn _— the etymological source of the global Western jasmine word and one of the most-praised aromatic flowers of the medieval Arabic-Persian pharmacy. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* ياسمين (_yāsamīn_) — the original Persian-Arabic name. Persian: یاسمن (_yāsmīn / yāsamīn_). Urdu/Hakeemi: یاسمین (_yāsmīn_); چنبیلی (_chambēlī_, Hindi-derived for _J. grandiflorum_); موتیا (_motiya_, for _J. sambac_).

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Moist in the 1st degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _yāsamīn_; some sources Hot-Dry 1st). The mild-warming classification *diverges from the Ayurvedic śīta vīrya* (cooling) — another cross-system divergence; the Tibb reading aligns with the perfumery-and-mood register, where jasmine is a warming-uplifting aromatic.

*Af'āl:* *Mufariḥ* (mood-elevating — the principal action; jasmine is one of the most-prized Mufarriḥ aromatics of Tibb); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī* (vital-spirit tonic); *muqawwī al-qalb* (cardiotonic); *muḥarrik al-bāh* (aphrodisiac); *muḥsin li-l-bashara* (skin-improving — the cosmetic register); *mufattiḥ al-ʿurūq* (vessel-opening); *muḥarrik al-ḥayḍ* (mild emmenagogue at high dose).

*Istemālāt:* *amrāḍ al-sawdā'* (melancholic / depressive conditions — the principal indication); cold-pattern weakness; cardiac weakness with emotional component; cosmetic skin preparations; mild headache from cold-melancholic pattern; impotence of cold pattern; sexual debility.

*Mawāne':* high fever with hot-pattern; pregnancy at high-essential-oil dose; the *Hot* mizāj makes it inappropriate for the most hot-pattern conditions.

*Muṣliḥ:* rose-water (the canonical floral pairing); camphor for hot constitutions.

*Badal:* rose; orange-blossom; champak (*champā*).

*Tibb formulas:* *ʿArq-i Yāsamīn* (jasmine distillate / hydrosol) — the canonical Unani distillate; *Rowghan-i Yāsamīn* (jasmine oil) — cosmetic and medical massage oil; *Khamīrah-i Marwārīd Khās* and other Mufarriḥ-class cardiacs use jasmine; *Sharbat-i Yāsamīn* — jasmine-flower syrup for mood and cardiac weakness; *Persian gulqand-class flower-jams* use jasmine in some recensions. The Mughal-Hakeemi cosmetic *ʿiṭr-i yāsamīn* (jasmine perfume) tradition is the direct ancestor of modern French jasmine-absolute perfumery.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _yāsamīn_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III; the Persian-Arabic perfumery literature (al-Kindī's *Kitāb Kīmiyā' al-ʿIṭr*, the foundational 9th-c. Arabic treatise on perfume distillation, treats jasmine extensively).#footnote[Tibb yāsamīn: Avicenna's classical treatment plus the Arabic perfumery tradition; the Tibb-Ayurvedic mizāj divergence (warming vs. cooling) is one of the standing classical disagreements.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *茉莉花茶 (mòlìhuāchá)* — *jasmine green tea*; see jasmine-tea.typ for the full treatment.
- *茉莉花飲 (Mòlìhuāyǐn)* — plain jasmine-flower infusion for Liver-qi stagnation with PMS; 3–6 g of dried flower.
- *Topical preparations* — jasmine flower decoction for eye washes, skin disease, mouth ulcers.
- *素馨花飲 (J. officinale / grandiflorum infusion)* — for skin disease and Liver-qi stagnation.
- *In compound formulas* — minor presence in modern Chinese herbal medicine; not in the major Shanghan Lun / Jinkui Yaolue tradition.

=== Ayurveda

- *Mallikā / jātī cūrṇa* — simple powdered dried flowers; 1–3 g; mood / cooling / mild digestive register.
- *Jātī-puṣpa-taila* — jasmine-flower-medicated oil; classical cosmetic / hair-tonic preparation; for skin tonification.
- *Jātī-pattra-kvātha (jasmine-leaf decoction)* — for chronic mouth ulcers, periodontal disease, sore throat — *the canonical Ayurvedic dental indication*.
- *Mallikā-arka* — distillate / hydrosol; used as Pitta-pacifying skin-toner and as gentle eye-wash in some traditions.
- *Mallikā in compound formulas* — adjuvant in _kuṣṭha-cikitsā_ (skin disease treatment) compound formulas.
- *Ritual / cosmetic / matrimonial use* — universal across Hindu South Asia: *mallikā mālā* (garlands) for women's hair, temple offerings, wedding ceremonies. The cosmetic-and-ritual register is *substantive*, not merely ornamental; the daily wearing of jasmine flowers in the hair is treated by the dravyaguna tradition as a *continuous low-dose aromatic-cosmetic-medical* application.#footnote[Ayurvedic compound and cosmetic preparations: _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_; _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I; standard Ayurvedic cosmetic literature (e.g. the classical _strī-bhūṣaṇa-prakaraṇa_ chapters). The everyday-wearing-jasmine register is one of the cleanest cases of dietary-and-cosmetic continuity in classical Indian medicine.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* jasmine is *not* in Dioscorides or Pliny; the species reached the Mediterranean only via Persian-Arabic transmission in the medieval period.
- *Medieval Arabic and European:* *Avicenna* (_Canon of Medicine_) treats _yāsamīn_ in the medical-pharmacy register — warming, drying, useful for cold-pattern complaints; this is the substantive medieval entry point of jasmine into European pharmacy.
- *Hildegard von Bingen* mentions jasmine briefly.
- *Renaissance Italian and French herbalism* — jasmine in early-modern pharmacy as fragrant flower-water (eau de jasmin) for cosmetic and mild medicinal use.
- *Galenic / pharmacy preparations:* _Aqua jasmini_, _Oleum jasmini_, _Pomatum jasmini_ (jasmine-pomade for hair); persisted into 19th-c. European pharmacy as cosmetic-aromatic preparations.
- *Modern Western pharmacy:* the substantive register is *perfumery*. *Jasmine absolute* (extracted by solvent extraction from _J. grandiflorum_, principally Grasse and Egypt) is *one of the foundational notes of modern French perfumery* — Chanel No. 5, Joy by Jean Patou, and an enormous fraction of fine perfumery use jasmine absolute as a core floral. The perfumery use is the principal modern Western commercial reception of jasmine.
- *Aromatherapy* — jasmine essential oil for mood, anxiety, mild depression; aromatherapy literature supports modest mood-elevating effects via inhalation.#footnote[Avicenna, _al-Qānūn fī-ṭ-ṭibb_, on _yāsamīn_; Hildegard, _Physica_ (Throop); standard pharmacy history (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_). Perfumery history: Grasse and the French perfume industry, late-19th-c. onward; modern reviews in _Perfumer & Flavorist_ and analytical literature on jasmine absolute.]

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Jasmine — across the species — is principally a *warm-climate* substance, with cultivation, ritual use, and medicinal use concentrated in *hot-humid* (South / Southeast Asian, Philippine), *subtropical-humid* (southern Chinese, Taiwanese, Hawaiian), and *Mediterranean-to-warm-arid* (Persian, Egyptian, southern European, Maghrebi) zones. See _climates.typ_ for the canonical climate framework.

*Hot-humid (South Asia and Southeast Asia — mallikā, jātī, jūhī, sampaguita, melati).* The South Asian monsoonal climate carries an annual Pitta-Kapha load with high _varṣā_ (monsoon, Caraka Sūtra 6) and _grīṣma_ (summer-heat) phases; in 中醫 terms (_Su Wen_ ch. 12) the southeastern register is 濕熱 (damp-heat) on the Heart and Spleen. *The traditional response is precisely the jasmine register*: cooling, calming, mild antimicrobial, aromatic-mood-elevating, applied at low daily dose through (a) *cosmetic / ritual flower wear* (garland in the hair — continuous low-dose aromatic Pitta-pacification), (b) *flower-water and floral hydrosol* for skin and ablutions (cooling), (c) *dental / oral preparations* (jasmine-flower mouthwash / leaf decoction), (d) *cosmetic-medical jasmine oils* (jātī-puṣpa-taila). The seasonal flowering of mallikā / jātī peaks in the monsoon and immediately post-monsoon — exactly when the Pitta-Kapha load is at maximum and when the constitutional correction is most needed. *Cares:* keep the flowers *fresh* (the volatile aromatic register decays rapidly — the famous evening-opening fragrance is the morning-flower's metabolism); avoid heavy-snigdha frying preparations that would clash with the light-aromatic register; pair with *cooling* foods (rice, mung dhal, coriander water) rather than warming-spice-heavy meals.

*Subtropical-humid (southern China and Taiwan — mòlìhuā).* The same climate logic that drives the South Asian use applies in southern China — damp-heat year-round with mild winters; the jasmine arrived with Buddhism in the Tang period and was integrated into the same climatic-constitutional register. The dominant local use is *tea-scenting* (see jasmine-tea.typ) — combining the cooling green-tea base with the aromatic flower for the locally-correct functional-medical register. The flower itself is also used in 茉莉花飲 and topical preparations as in South Asia.

*Mediterranean and Persian (J. officinale, J. grandiflorum).* The Persian heritage range (Iran, Afghanistan) is at the *cold-dry / continental* edge for jasmine cultivation; the species _J. officinale_ is the cold-hardier of the genus and is the Mediterranean / Persian classical jasmine. The climate is more *warm-dry-summer / cool-wet-winter* (Mediterranean) rather than hot-humid; the constitutional reading shifts subtly — the same cooling-aromatic register is appropriate for the *hot-dry summer* phase but the substance is *out of season* in the wet winter. Persian and Mediterranean use concentrated jasmine in *cosmetic, perfumery, and culinary-syrup* preparations (sharbat-e yāsamīn, jasmine syrup) — appropriate to the summer-load but moderate in the winter. *Cares:* the dry climate calls for the flower to be paired with *snigdha-cooling* vehicles (rose-water, milk, almond-oil) rather than the simpler infusion that suffices in hot-humid climates.

*Hot-arid / Saharan and North African (J. grandiflorum cultivation for perfumery — Egypt, Morocco).* The substance shifts register from medicine to *perfumery commodity*. Cultivation requires irrigation; the flowers concentrate aromatic compounds in the heat. The Maghrebi and Egyptian use *jasmine flower water* (in _eau de fleur d'oranger_-style preparations) and *jasmine in mint tea*; the climatic register is the same hot-day cooling-aromatic logic.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Jasmine flowers exported as *cut flowers* and *floral garnish* to cool-damp Northern European or cold-dry continental contexts are out of climatic register; the substance is not constitutional medicine in those contexts but ornamental commodity. The *perfumery* register (jasmine absolute, jasmine essential oil) transcends climate by abstraction — the volatile fraction is consumed via inhalation, which is climate-independent in pharmacological effect (the limbic / olfactory pathway). The *aromatherapy* practice of using diluted jasmine essential oil for mood is climatically-neutral and may be the most legitimate trans-climate application. *Cold-pattern winter consumption of jasmine tea in cool-damp Atlantic European contexts* is mild _kāla-viruddha_: the substance is fine but is functionally aimed at a constitutional load that is not the local one; warming root vegetables and fermented sour preparations would be the locally-correct register, with jasmine tea as a subordinate guest substance.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents (jasmine flower volatile oil):*
- *Benzyl acetate* — ~25–60% of the volatile fraction in _J. sambac_; the principal jasmine aroma compound.
- *Linalool* — ~5–15%; well-characterized anxiolytic / mild sedative.
- *Indole* — ~1–2%; the distinctive intense floral / faintly-animalic note in deep concentration; in trace amounts is essential for "real jasmine" character.
- *Methyl anthranilate* — characteristic; "grape" floral note.
- *Methyl jasmonate, jasmone* — important jasmonate-class compounds; modulators of plant signaling, with modest pharmacology.
- *Eugenol* (trace), *geraniol*, *nerolidol*, *cis-3-hexenyl benzoate*.
- *Phenylpropanoids* — additional aromatic spectrum.

_Jasminum grandiflorum_ has higher proportions of *jasmone, methyl jasmonate, and cis-jasmone* — distinctive for perfumery; _J. sambac_ has more benzyl acetate and indole.#footnote[Phytochemistry of jasmine flower volatile oil: standard analytical literature; reviews in _Phytochemistry_, _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_, _Flavour and Fragrance Journal_. The _J. sambac_ vs. _J. grandiflorum_ chemical distinction is well-characterized and is the basis of their different perfumery applications.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Anxiolytic / mild sedative*: linalool is the principal mechanistic contributor; well-characterized GABA-A potentiation and limbic modulation. Clinical evidence for jasmine inhalation in mild anxiety is modest-to-moderate.
- *Mood-elevating*: olfactory-limbic pathway; aromatherapy literature supports modest mood-elevating effects.
- *Antimicrobial*: benzyl acetate and other components have broad in vitro activity; clinical relevance for the classical dental-mouthwash use is modest-supported.
- *Mild antispasmodic*: methyl jasmonate and related compounds; modest preclinical.
- *Anti-inflammatory*: preclinical.
- *Anti-cancer (preclinical)*: jasmine essential oil and methyl jasmonate have shown apoptosis-inducing activity in some cancer-cell lines; clinical translation preliminary.
- *Galactagogue*: traditional use; modest folk-medical attribution; clinical evidence weak.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Chinese 理氣解鬱 + Ayurvedic _manas-prasādana_ + modern linalool/methyl-jasmonate pharmacology converge on the *mood-elevating, anxiolytic, mild sedative* register; one of the cleaner cross-system convergences in floral pharmacy.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Dried flower*: 3–6 g/day in tea / infusion; well-tolerated.
- *Jasmine flower water (hydrosol)*: cosmetic and dietary use; well-tolerated.
- *Jasmine essential oil*: *concentrated* — *not for unsupervised oral use*; topical use at low dilution (~0.5–2% in carrier oil); inhalation safe and the standard aromatherapy register.
- *Jasmine absolute (perfumery)*: not for ingestion (contains solvent residues from extraction); cosmetic / olfactory use only.
- *Risk groups*: (a) *pregnancy* — avoid essential oil internally (precautionary); ritual / culinary / aromatic use fine; (b) jasmine allergy (rare); (c) concurrent sedative therapy at high-dose essential oil; (d) infants / pediatric — avoid concentrated essential oil; ritual use fine.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

